          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exploring Algebraic Identities"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.855,
            0.068,
            0.883,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "79"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.106,
            0.496,
            0.342
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 2,
              "bbox": [
                0.156,
                0.106,
                0.496,
                0.342
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_11_image_2.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.534,
            0.126,
            0.885,
            0.359
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 4.7 helps to visualise the product of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x \\quad + \\quad 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x \\; + \\; 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " using algebra tiles. The expression "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x + 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is represented using an x-tile and three unit tiles. Similarly "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x \\quad + \\quad 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is represented using an x-tile and four unit tiles. The product of these two linear factors is shown in the inner rectangle comprising the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x \\mathrm { { - } t i l e } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the x-tiles, and 12 unit tiles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.355,
            0.487,
            0.374
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 4.7: Factorisation o "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f x ^ { 2 } + 7 x + 1 2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.383,
            0.887,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note that the 7x in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x^{2} + 7x + 12"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " has been split as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3x \\pm 4x."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " This is represented by the fact that three x-tiles are placed on the right side of the x"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "-tile and four x-tiles are arranged below it. Also, the 12 unit tiles are arranged in a 3 by 4 array. Once the rectangular arrangement is formed, we observe that the dimensions of the rectangle are "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x + 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x + 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " units respectively."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.517,
            0.399,
            0.54
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.553,
            0.855,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Suppose 7x is split as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2x + 5x;"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " can a similar rectangular arrangement be formed? Consider other possibilities and check."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.606,
            0.718,
            0.628
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 4.7 helps us to visualise two algebraic identities:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.635,
            0.883,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) The linear expressions "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x \\mp 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x + 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " can be multiplied to obtain the identity "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(x + 3)(x + 4) = x^{2} + 7x + 12."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.68,
            0.884,
            0.742
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Also the expression "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x^{2} + 7x + 12"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " can be factored into the linear factors "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( x   +   3 )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( x   +   4 )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , giving the same identity "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x^{2} + 7x + 12 ="
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(x + 3)(x + 4)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.756,
            0.396,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.794,
            0.768,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Algebra tiles can be used to represent products and find factors."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.816,
            0.765,
            0.837
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Figure out the product of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x + 2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x + 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " using algebra tiles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.84,
            0.854,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Lay out algebra tiles for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x^{2} + 11x + 30"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in such a way that you will see its factors."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
